112 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 77 0 R
/E (An unmanned aerial vehicle \(UAV\), commonly called a drone, is an aircraft that flies without a human pilot on board. It is controlled either remotely by a person on the ground or autonomously by onboard computers that follow a programmed mission. Drones are used today in aerial photography, agriculture, surveying and mapping, parcel delivery, infrastructure inspection, search and rescue, firefighting, defence, and academic research. Behind all these applications is a small but very capable embedded computer system that senses the environment, makes decisions, and drives the motors hundreds of times every second.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
113 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 77 0 R
/E (Computer organization deals with the operational units of a computer and their interconnections: how the processor, memory, input/output devices and buses are arranged and how they cooperate to execute instructions. It is closely related to digital principles, because every unit inside a computer is built from logic gates, flip-flops, registers, counters, multiplexers and arithmetic circuits. A drone is an excellent case study for both subjects because it is a complete real-time digital system in which these building blocks are clearly visible.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
114 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 77 0 R
/E (A typical multirotor drone contains a flight controller, which is a microcontroller-based computer board. The flight controller reads data from sensors such as the gyroscope, accelerometer, magnetometer, barometer and GPS receiver. It processes this data using a fixed-point or floating-point processor, compares the current state of the aircraft with the desired state, and calculates the speed required for each motor. The result is sent as digital pulses to the Electronic Speed Controllers \(ESCs\), which drive the brushless motors. Unlike a general-purpose computer or a smartphone, a drone computer must meet strict real-time deadlines. If the control calculation is delayed even by a few milliseconds, the aircraft can become unstable and crash. The system must also be light, consume little power, tolerate vibration and temperature variation, and continue to operate safely when a sensor or communication link fails.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
115 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 77 0 R
/E (Advanced drones also carry a second computer called a companion computer. It runs heavy tasks such as computer vision, obstacle avoidance, object tracking, mapping and machine-learning inference, while the flight controller remains responsible for stability. This two-level organization is a practical example of dividing work among specialized processors, an idea that also appears in the System-on-Chip designs of modern smartphones.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
116 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 77 0 R
/E (This case study examines the computer organization of drones: the processor, memory, input/output, buses, timers, sensors, software and power system, and shows how the principles of digital logic and computer organization are applied in a real flying machine.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
