/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 78 0 R
/T (The history of unmanned aircraft goes back to the early twentieth century, when simple radio-controlled aircraft were built mainly as military target vehicles. Their control systems were analog: relays, vacuum tubes and, later, discrete transistor circuits. The pilot's commands were converted directly into movement of the control surfaces, and there was no onboard computation of any significant kind.)
/E (The history of unmanned aircraft goes back to the early twentieth century, when simple radio-controlled aircraft were built mainly as military target vehicles. Their control systems were analog: relays, vacuum tubes and, later, discrete transistor circuits. The pilot's commands were converted directly into movement of the control surfaces, and there was no onboard computation of any significant kind.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
139 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 78 0 R
/T (The arrival of the microprocessor and later the microcontroller changed this situation. Small digital processors could read sensors, execute control algorithms and generate output signals on a single chip. The development of MEMS \(Micro-Electro-Mechanical Systems\) sensors, which place a gyroscope and accelerometer on a tiny silicon chip, allowed an inertial measurement unit to weigh only a fraction of a gram. Together with high-energy-density lithium-polymer batteries and efficient brushless motors, these technologies made small multirotor drones practical and affordable. Since a quadcopter is naturally unstable, it can fly only because a computer continuously corrects its attitude. Modern flight controllers are built around 32-bit ARM Cortex-M microcontrollers, commonly from the Cortex-M4, M7 and similar families, running at clock frequencies of the order of a few hundred megahertz. Open-source autopilot projects such as ArduPilot and PX4 run on these boards, and many commercial drones use similar designs. For vision and AI tasks, drones increasingly carry a companion computer based on a multi-core application processor with a GPU, such as a single-board computer or an embedded AI module.)
/E (The arrival of the microprocessor and later the microcontroller changed this situation. Small digital processors could read sensors, execute control algorithms and generate output signals on a single chip. The development of MEMS \(Micro-Electro-Mechanical Systems\) sensors, which place a gyroscope and accelerometer on a tiny silicon chip, allowed an inertial measurement unit to weigh only a fraction of a gram. Together with high-energy-density lithium-polymer batteries and efficient brushless motors, these technologies made small multirotor drones practical and affordable. Since a quadcopter is naturally unstable, it can fly only because a computer continuously corrects its attitude. Modern flight controllers are built around 32-bit ARM Cortex-M microcontrollers, commonly from the Cortex-M4, M7 and similar families, running at clock frequencies of the order of a few hundred megahertz. Open-source autopilot projects such as ArduPilot and PX4 run on these boards, and many commercial drones use similar designs. For vision and AI tasks, drones increasingly carry a companion computer based on a multi-core application processor with a GPU, such as a single-board computer or an embedded AI module.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
