176 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 79 0 R
/T (The flight controller is the heart of this system. It follows the stored-program concept of the von Neumann computer: instructions are fetched from memory, decoded by the control unit, and executed by the arithmetic and logic unit \(ALU\), and the results are stored in registers or memory. Most microcontroller cores use a modified Harvard organization, with separate paths for instructions and data inside the chip, so that the next instruction can be fetched while data from the previous one is being accessed.)
/E (The flight controller is the heart of this system. It follows the stored-program concept of the von Neumann computer: instructions are fetched from memory, decoded by the control unit, and executed by the arithmetic and logic unit \(ALU\), and the results are stored in registers or memory. Most microcontroller cores use a modified Harvard organization, with separate paths for instructions and data inside the chip, so that the next instruction can be fetched while data from the previous one is being accessed.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [34]
>>
endobj
177 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 79 0 R
/T (A second important background idea is that of the embedded system. A drone computer has a fixed purpose and is permanently built into the machine it controls. It has no keyboard or monitor and typically no conventional operating system. Its software is designed around the hardware, and its performance is judged by determinism and reliability rather than by raw speed.)
/E (A second important background idea is that of the embedded system. A drone computer has a fixed purpose and is permanently built into the machine it controls. It has no keyboard or monitor and typically no conventional operating system. Its software is designed around the hardware, and its performance is judged by determinism and reliability rather than by raw speed.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [35]
>>
endobj
