/T (A key feature for a drone is the floating-point unit \(FPU\). Control algorithms and sensor fusion involve many multiplications, divisions, trigonometric functions and square roots on fractional numbers. An FPU executes single-precision floating-point operations in hardware in a few clock cycles, whereas a processor without one must emulate them in software, which is many times slower. Hardware multiply-accumulate and DSP instructions further speed up filters and matrix calculations.)
/E (A key feature for a drone is the floating-point unit \(FPU\). Control algorithms and sensor fusion involve many multiplications, divisions, trigonometric functions and square roots on fractional numbers. An FPU executes single-precision floating-point operations in hardware in a few clock cycles, whereas a processor without one must emulate them in software, which is many times slower. Hardware multiply-accumulate and DSP instructions further speed up filters and matrix calculations.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [26]
>>
endobj
254 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 82 0 R
/T (Another important feature is the interrupt controller. The CPU does not continuously check each device \(polling\). Instead, a device signals the CPU through an interrupt request, the CPU saves its present state, executes a short interrupt service routine, and then resumes the interrupted work. Interrupts are used when a new IMU sample is ready, when a byte is received from the GPS, or when a timer period has elapsed.)
/E (Another important feature is the interrupt controller. The CPU does not continuously check each device \(polling\). Instead, a device signals the CPU through an interrupt request, the CPU saves its present state, executes a short interrupt service routine, and then resumes the interrupted work. Interrupts are used when a new IMU sample is ready, when a byte is received from the GPS, or when a timer period has elapsed.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [27]
>>
endobj
