300 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 84 0 R
/E (Timers and PWM. A hardware timer is a binary counter driven by the clock. A comparator compares the counter with a stored value, and the output is set high or low when they match. This produces a pulse-width-modulated \(PWM\) signal whose width is set by software but generated entirely in hardware, so there is no jitter caused by software execution. Conventional ESC signals use pulse widths of about 1 ms to 2 ms to represent zero to full throttle. Newer digital protocols such as DShot send the throttle value as a digital word, which is immune to analog timing errors.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
301 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 84 0 R
/E (Number representation. Sensor values are stored as signed binary numbers in two's complement form. The raw output of a 16-bit gyroscope, for example, is a signed integer that is scaled by the sensitivity setting to give an angular rate in degrees per second. The controller converts between integer and floating-point formats when required.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
302 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 84 0 R
/E (Clock. A crystal oscillator and phase-locked loop supply the system clock. All state changes in the processor take place on clock edges, which is the basis of synchronous sequential design. The clock frequency determines the instruction rate, and a stable clock is essential for accurate timing and communication baud rates.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
303 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
