/E (6. Software and Real-Time Operation)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
304 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 84 0 R
/E (The firmware of a flight controller is normally built on a real-time operating system \(RTOS\) or a carefully scheduled bare-metal loop. Open-source autopilots use RTOS kernels that provide tasks, priorities, timers and message passing. The most urgent task, which is the attitude and rate control loop, has the highest priority. Less urgent tasks such as logging, telemetry and navigation run at lower priority and are pre-empted when necessary.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
305 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 84 0 R
/E (The central control computation, shown in Figure 4, is a repeated sequence. Sensor data are read, a state estimator combines gyroscope, accelerometer, magnetometer, barometer and GPS data to calculate attitude, velocity and position, and PID controllers calculate the correction required. A motor mixer converts the desired thrust and torques into four individual motor commands, which are sent to the ESCs. The inner rate loop typically runs at hundreds to several thousand times per second, while position and navigation loops run more slowly.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
