379 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (The computer organization of drones is an excellent example of how the theory of digital principles and computer organization is applied in a real embedded system. A drone combines a CPU, memory, input and output devices, buses, timers, a clock and a power system in a small and light platform that must operate in real time. Each unit has a clear purpose, and the interconnections between them follow well-defined digital interfaces.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
380 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (The flight controller, built around a 32-bit microcontroller with a floating-point unit, reads sensors through SPI, I2C and UART, estimates the state of the aircraft using sensor fusion, calculates corrections with PID controllers and sends precise motor commands through hardware timers. Interrupts, DMA and a real-time operating system make sure that the deadlines of the control loop are always met. The companion computer adds vision and artificial intelligence without disturbing the safety-critical loop.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
381 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (The most important challenge in drone architecture is balancing performance against power, weight, cost, heat and reliability. Features such as dedicated hardware peripherals, efficient memory organization, regulated power supply, watchdog timers and redundant sensors help achieve this balance and make the system safe.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
382 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (Drone computer organization also shows the strong link between hardware and software. The firmware is written closely around the hardware, and its performance is limited and enabled by the structure of the processor, memory and peripherals. As drones are increasingly used for delivery, agriculture, inspection and autonomous missions, their computer organization will continue to develop, with faster processors, more specialized accelerators and stronger security.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
383 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (In conclusion, a drone is a flying computer. Studying its organization helps students understand how logic gates, registers, counters, memory, buses and processors combine to form a complete working system that can sense, think and act in real time.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
