/Title (The arrival of the microprocessor and later the microcontroller changed this situation. Small digital processors could read sensors, execute control algorithms and generate output signals on a single chip. The development of MEMS \(Micro-Electro-Mechanical Systems\) sensors, which place a gyroscope and accelerometer on a tiny silicon chip, allowed an inertial measurement unit to weigh only a fraction of a gram. Together with high-energy-density lithium-polymer batteries and efficient brushless motors, these technologies made small multirotor drones practical and affordable. Since a quadcopter is naturally unstable, it can fly only because a computer continuously corrects its attitude. Modern flight controllers are built around 32-bit ARM Cortex-M microcontrollers, commonly from the Cortex-M4, M7 and similar families, running at clock frequencies of the order of a few hundred megahertz. Open-source autopilot projects such as ArduPilot and PX4 run on these boards, and many commercial drones use similar designs. For vision and AI tasks, drones increasingly carry a companion computer based on a multi-core application processor with a GPU, such as a single-board computer or an embedded AI module.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 669 0 R
/Prev 773 0 R
/Next 841 0 R
>>
endobj
831 0 obj
<<
/Title (The flight controller is the heart of this system. It follows the stored-program concept of the von Neumann computer: instructions are fetched from memory, decoded by the control unit, and executed by the arithmetic and logic unit \(ALU\), and the results are stored in registers or memory. Most microcontroller cores use a modified Harvard organization, with separate paths for instructions and data inside the chip, so that the next instruction can be fetched while data from the previous one is being accessed.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 669 0 R
/Prev 841 0 R
/Next 774 0 R
>>
endobj
