/Title (When a drone must interpret camera images, build a map or avoid obstacles, the processing requirement is far above what a microcontroller can supply. The drone therefore adds a companion computer, which is normally a multi-core ARM processor with a GPU and sometimes a neural processing unit \(NPU\). It runs a full operating system such as Linux and communicates with the flight controller through a UART or Ethernet link, using a lightweight message protocol. The companion computer sends high-level commands such as velocity or waypoint setpoints, but the flight controller always keeps the final responsibility for stability.)
/Dest [19 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 834 0 R
/Next 849 0 R
>>
endobj
849 0 obj
<<
/Title (This arrangement shows the same principle seen in smartphone SoCs: specialized hardware, such as a GPU or NPU, can execute a certain class of workload far more efficiently than a general-purpose CPU. It also isolates the safety-critical control loop from non-deterministic software. If the companion computer crashes, the flight controller can still hold the aircraft stable or return to land.)
/Dest [19 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 834 0 R
/Prev 848 0 R
>>
endobj
